DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ggeellii utility is used to configure encryption on GEOM providers.

     The following is a list of the most important features:

           ••   Utilizes the crypto(9) framework, so when there is crypto hard‐
               ware available, ggeellii will make use of it automatically.
           ••   Supports many cryptographic algorithms (currently AAEESS--XXTTSS,
               AAEESS--CCBBCC, and CCaammeelllliiaa--CCBBCC).
           ••   Can optionally perform data authentication (integrity verifica‐
               tion) utilizing one of the following algorithms: HHMMAACC//SSHHAA11,
               HHMMAACC//RRIIPPEEMMDD116600, HHMMAACC//SSHHAA225566, HHMMAACC//SSHHAA338844 or HHMMAACC//SSHHAA551122.
           ••   Can create a User Key from up to two, piecewise components: a
               passphrase entered via prompt or read from one or more pass‐
               files; a keyfile read from one or more files.
           ••   Allows encryption of the root partition.  The user is asked for
               the passphrase before the root filesystem is mounted.
           ••   Strengthens the passphrase component of the User Key with: B.
               Kaliski, _P_K_C_S _#_5_: _P_a_s_s_w_o_r_d_-_B_a_s_e_d _C_r_y_p_t_o_g_r_a_p_h_y _S_p_e_c_i_f_i_c_a_t_i_o_n_,
               _V_e_r_s_i_o_n _2_._0_., RFC, 2898.
           ••   Allows the use of two independent User Keys (e.g., a "user key"
               and a "company key").
           ••   It is fast - ggeellii performs simple sector-to-sector encryption.
           ••   Allows the encrypted Master Key to be backed up and restored,
               so that if a user has to quickly destroy key material, it is
               possible to get the data back by restoring keys from backup.
           ••   Providers can be configured to automatically detach on last
               close, so users do not have to remember to detach providers af‐
               ter unmounting the filesystems.
           ••   Allows attaching a provider with a random, one-time Master Key,
               which is useful for swap partitions and temporary filesystems.
           ••   Allows verification of data integrity (data authentication).
           ••   Allows suspending and resuming encrypted devices.

     The first argument to ggeellii indicates an action to be performed:

     iinniitt       Initialize providers which need to be encrypted.  If multiple
                providers are listed as arguments, they will all be initial‐
                ized with the same passphrase and/or User Key.  A unique salt
                will be randomly generated for each provider to ensure the
                Master Key for each is unique.  Here you can set up the cryp‐
                tographic algorithm to use, Data Key length, etc.  The last
                sector of the providers is used to store metadata.  The iinniitt
                subcommand also automatically writes metadata backups to
                _/_v_a_r_/_b_a_c_k_u_p_s_/_<_p_r_o_v_>_._e_l_i file.  The metadata can be recovered
                with the rreessttoorree subcommand described below.

                Additional options include:

                --aa _a_a_l_g_o        Enable data integrity verification (authenti‐
                                cation) using the given algorithm.  This will
                                reduce the size of storage available and also
                                reduce speed.  For example, when using 4096
                                bytes sector and HHMMAACC//SSHHAA225566 algorithm, 89% of
                                the original provider storage will be avail‐
                                able for use.  Currently supported algorithms
                                are: HHMMAACC//SSHHAA11, HHMMAACC//RRIIPPEEMMDD116600, HHMMAACC//SSHHAA225566,
                                HHMMAACC//SSHHAA338844 and HHMMAACC//SSHHAA551122.  If the option is
                                not given, there will be no authentication,
                                only encryption.  The recommended algorithm is
                                HHMMAACC//SSHHAA225566.

                --bb              Try to decrypt this partition during boot, be‐
                                fore the root partition is mounted.  This
                                makes it possible to use an encrypted root
                                partition.  One will still need bootable unen‐
                                crypted storage with a _/_b_o_o_t_/ directory, which
                                can be a CD-ROM disc or USB pen-drive, that
                                can be removed after boot.

                --BB _b_a_c_k_u_p_f_i_l_e   File name to use for metadata backup instead
                                of the default _/_v_a_r_/_b_a_c_k_u_p_s_/_<_p_r_o_v_>_._e_l_i.  To
                                inhibit backups, you can use _n_o_n_e as the
                                _b_a_c_k_u_p_f_i_l_e.  If multiple providers were ini‐
                                tialized in the one command, you can use _P_R_O_V
                                (all upper-case) in the file name, and it will
                                be replaced with the provider name.  If _P_R_O_V
                                is not found in the file name and multiple
                                providers were initialized in the one command,
                                _-_<_p_r_o_v_> will be appended to the end of the
                                file name specified.

                --dd              When entering the passphrase to boot from this
                                encrypted root filesystem, echo ‘*’ charac‐
                                ters.  This makes the length of the passphrase
                                visible.

                --ee _e_a_l_g_o        Encryption algorithm to use.  Currently sup‐
                                ported algorithms are: AAEESS--XXTTSS, AAEESS--CCBBCC,
                                CCaammeelllliiaa--CCBBCC, and NNUULLLL.  The default and rec‐
                                ommended algorithm is AAEESS--XXTTSS.  NNUULLLL is unen‐
                                crypted.

                --gg              Enable booting from this encrypted root
                                filesystem.  The boot loader prompts for the
                                passphrase and loads loader(8) from the en‐
                                crypted partition.

                --ii _i_t_e_r_a_t_i_o_n_s   Number of iterations to use with PKCS#5v2 when
                                processing User Key passphrase component.  If
                                this option is not specified, ggeellii will find
                                the number of iterations which is equal to 2
                                seconds of crypto work.  If 0 is given,
                                PKCS#5v2 will not be used.  PKCS#5v2 process‐
                                ing is performed once, after all parts of the
                                passphrase component have been read.

                --JJ _n_e_w_p_a_s_s_f_i_l_e  Specifies a file which contains the passphrase
                                component of the User Key (or part of it).  If
                                _n_e_w_p_a_s_s_f_i_l_e is given as -, standard input will
                                be used.  Only the first line (excluding new-
                                line character) is taken from the given file.
                                This argument can be specified multiple times,
                                which has the effect of reassembling a single
                                passphrase split across multiple files.  Can‐
                                not be combined with the --PP option.

                --KK _n_e_w_k_e_y_f_i_l_e   Specifies a file which contains the keyfile
                                component of the User Key (or part of it).  If
                                _n_e_w_k_e_y_f_i_l_e is given as -, standard input will
                                be used.  This argument can be specified mul‐
                                tiple times, which has the effect of reassem‐
                                bling a single keyfile split across multiple
                                keyfile parts.

                --ll _k_e_y_l_e_n       Data Key length to use with the given crypto‐
                                graphic algorithm.  If the length is not spec‐
                                ified, the selected algorithm uses its _d_e_f_a_u_l_t
                                key length.

                                      AAEESS--XXTTSS
                                      _1_2_8, 256

                                      AAEESS--CCBBCC, CCaammeelllliiaa--CCBBCC
                                      _1_2_8, 192, 256

                --PP              Do not use a passphrase as a component of the
                                User Key.  Cannot be combined with the --JJ op‐
                                tion.

                --ss _s_e_c_t_o_r_s_i_z_e   Change decrypted provider's sector size.  In‐
                                creasing the sector size allows increased per‐
                                formance, because encryption/decryption which
                                requires an initialization vector is done per
                                sector; fewer sectors means less computational
                                work.

                --RR              Turn off automatic expansion.  By default, if
                                the underlying provider grows, the encrypted
                                provider will grow automatically too.  The
                                metadata will be moved to the new location.
                                If automatic expansion if turned off and the
                                underlying provider changes size, attaching
                                encrypted provider will no longer be possible
                                as the metadata will no longer be located in
                                the last sector.  In this case GGEELLII will only
                                log the previous size of the underlying
                                provider, so metadata can be found easier, if
                                resize was done by mistake.

                --TT              Don't pass through BIO_DELETE calls (i.e.,
                                TRIM/UNMAP).  This can prevent an attacker
                                from knowing how much space you're actually
                                using and which sectors contain live data, but
                                will also prevent the backing store (SSD, etc)
                                from reclaiming space you're not using, which
                                may degrade its performance and lifespan.  The
                                underlying provider may or may not actually
                                obliterate the deleted sectors when TRIM is
                                enabled, so it should not be considered to add
                                any security.

                --VV _v_e_r_s_i_o_n      Metadata version to use.  This option is help‐
                                ful when creating a provider that may be used
                                by older FFrreeeeBBSSDD//GGEELLII versions.  Consult the
                                _H_I_S_T_O_R_Y section to find which metadata version
                                is supported by which FreeBSD version.  Note
                                that using an older version of metadata may
                                limit the number of features available.

     aattttaacchh     Attach the given providers.  The encrypted Master Keys are
                loaded from the metadata and decrypted using the given
                passphrase/keyfile and new GEOM providers are created using
                the specified provider names.  A ".eli" suffix is added to the
                user specified provider names.  Multiple providers can only be
                attached with a single aattttaacchh command if they all have the
                same passphrase and keyfiles.

                Additional options include:

                --CC           Do a dry-run decryption.  This is useful to ver‐
                             ify passphrase and keyfile without decrypting the
                             device.

                --dd           If specified, the decrypted providers are de‐
                             tached automatically on last close, so the user
                             does not have to remember to detach providers af‐
                             ter unmounting the filesystems.  This only works
                             when providers were opened for writing, and will
                             not work if the filesystems on the providers were
                             mounted read-only.  Probably a better choice is
                             the --ll option for the ddeettaacchh subcommand.

                --nn _k_e_y_n_o     Specifies the index number of the Master Key copy
                             to use (could be 0 or 1).  If the index number is
                             not provided all keys will be tested.

                --jj _p_a_s_s_f_i_l_e  Specifies a file which contains the passphrase
                             component of the User Key (or part of it).  For
                             more information see the description of the --JJ
                             option for the iinniitt subcommand.  The same pass‐
                             files are used for all listed providers.

                --kk _k_e_y_f_i_l_e   Specifies a file which contains the keyfile com‐
                             ponent of the User Key (or part of it).  For more
                             information see the description of the --KK option
                             for the iinniitt subcommand.  The same keyfiles are
                             used for all listed providers.

                --pp           Do not use a passphrase as a component of the
                             User Keys.  Cannot be combined with the --jj op‐
                             tion.

                --rr           Attach read-only providers.  They are not opened
                             for writing.

     ddeettaacchh     Detach the given providers, which means remove the devfs entry
                and clear the Master Key and Data Keys from memory.

                Additional options include:

                --ff  Force detach - detach even if the provider is open.

                --ll  Mark provider to detach on last close, after the last
                    filesystem has been unmounted.  If this option is speci‐
                    fied, the provider will not be detached while it is open,
                    but will be automatically detached when it is closed for
                    the last time even if it was only opened for reading.

     oonneettiimmee    Attach the given providers with a random, one-time (ephemeral)
                Master Key.  The command can be used to encrypt swap parti‐
                tions or temporary filesystems.

                Additional options include:

                --aa _a_a_l_g_o       Enable data integrity verification (authentica‐
                               tion).  For more information, see the descrip‐
                               tion of the iinniitt subcommand.

                --ee _e_a_l_g_o       Encryption algorithm to use.  For more informa‐
                               tion, see the description of the iinniitt subcom‐
                               mand.

                --dd             Detach on last close, after the last filesystem
                               has been unmounted.  Note: this option is not
                               usable for temporary filesystems as the
                               provider is detached after the filesystem has
                               been created.  It still can, and should, be
                               used for swap partitions.  For more informa‐
                               tion, see the description of the aattttaacchh subcom‐
                               mand.

                --ll _k_e_y_l_e_n      Data Key length to use with the given crypto‐
                               graphic algorithm.  For more information, see
                               the description of the iinniitt subcommand.

                --ss _s_e_c_t_o_r_s_i_z_e  Change decrypted provider's sector size.  For
                               more information, see the description of the
                               iinniitt subcommand.

                --RR             Turn off automatic expansion.  For more infor‐
                               mation, see the description of the iinniitt subcom‐
                               mand.

                --TT             Disable TRIM/UNMAP passthru.  For more informa‐
                               tion, see the description of the iinniitt subcom‐
                               mand.

     ccoonnffiigguurree  Change configuration of the given providers.

                Additional options include:

                --bb  Set the BOOT flag on the given providers.  For more infor‐
                    mation, see the description of the iinniitt subcommand.

                --BB  Remove the BOOT flag from the given providers.

                --dd  When entering the passphrase to boot from this encrypted
                    root filesystem, echo ‘*’ characters.  This makes the
                    length of the passphrase visible.

                --DD  Disable echoing of any characters when a passphrase is en‐
                    tered to boot from this encrypted root filesystem.  This
                    hides the passphrase length.

                --gg  Enable booting from this encrypted root filesystem.  The
                    boot loader prompts for the passphrase and loads loader(8)
                    from the encrypted partition.

                --GG  Deactivate booting from this encrypted root partition.

                --rr  Turn on automatic expansion.  For more information, see
                    the description of the iinniitt subcommand.

                --RR  Turn off automatic expansion.

                --tt  Enable TRIM/UNMAP passthru.  For more information, see the
                    description of the iinniitt subcommand.

                --TT  Disable TRIM/UNMAP passthru.

     sseettkkeeyy     Install a copy of the Master Key into the selected slot, en‐
                crypted with a new User Key.  If the selected slot is popu‐
                lated, replace the existing copy.  A provider has one Master
                Key, which can be stored in one or both slots, each encrypted
                with an independent User Key.  With the iinniitt subcommand, only
                key number 0 is initialized.  The User Key can be changed at
                any time: for an attached provider, for a detached provider,
                or on the backup file.  When a provider is attached, the user
                does not have to provide an existing passphrase/keyfile.

                Additional options include:

                --ii _i_t_e_r_a_t_i_o_n_s   Number of iterations to use with PKCS#5v2.  If
                                0 is given, PKCS#5v2 will not be used.  To be
                                able to use this option with the sseettkkeeyy sub‐
                                command, only one key has to be defined and
                                this key must be changed.

                --jj _p_a_s_s_f_i_l_e     Specifies a file which contains the passphrase
                                component of a current User Key (or part of
                                it).

                --JJ _n_e_w_p_a_s_s_f_i_l_e  Specifies a file which contains the passphrase
                                component of the new User Key (or part of it).

                --kk _k_e_y_f_i_l_e      Specifies a file which contains the keyfile
                                component of a current User Key (or part of
                                it).

                --KK _n_e_w_k_e_y_f_i_l_e   Specifies a file which contains the keyfile
                                component of the new User Key (or part of it).

                --nn _k_e_y_n_o        Specifies the index number of the Master Key
                                copy to change (could be 0 or 1).  If the
                                provider is attached and no key number is
                                given, the key used for attaching the provider
                                will be changed.  If the provider is detached
